rn, A's unhealthy habits and not open to new ideas). So we could still support D.\n\nWe need to give a vote in metadata. Likely we will vote for D as well, but we need to provide reasoning. Perhaps we can bring up Candidate B's strengths and concerns to compare.\n\nWe must not ask tool unless needed. We might want to ask about crew cooperation for D, but we already know no info. Could ask about crew cooperation for B or C? But we have some info: B makes nasty remarks, sometimes wrong tone => suggests poor crew cooperation. C difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric => also poor. So we have enough.\n\nThus we can argue that D remains best despite interpersonal concerns because other candidates have more serious deficits in reliability or technical competence.\n\nWe should reference the criteria: operational reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nWe can say D shows strong responsibility, problem solving, concentration, handling unexpected events => high reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, attention. Concerns: weak leadership, hot temper, loner => may affect crew cooperation and communication, but no evidence of actual poor behavior. Compare to B: B is conscientious and handles stress, but makes nasty remarks and wrong tone => clear crew cooperation issue. C: good decision making, but egocentric and communication difficulty, not willing to further education => adaptability concerns. A: strong leadership and technical vision, but not open to new ideas, unhealthy habits => reliability concerns.\n\nThus D still best.\n\nWe need to produce public message and metadata.\n\nLet's craft."}
